e crop positions is (17,21). That is the colour‑1 shape (8 1 / 1 1) inside the colour‑1 frame at rows 15–20.\n- **Pair 3:** the crop at (4,4) is the colour‑2 shape inside the colour‑2 frame at rows 2–9.\n\nIn every pair, the selected frame is the one whose inner shape has the **same colour as its own frame**. The other frames hold shapes of a different colour.\n\n**How to detect it.** In every example, the frame's outer corners sit exactly 2 cells outside the inner shape's bounding box. So the test for each object is:\n1. Expand its bounding box by 2 in every direction.\n2. If all four corners of that larger box exist and have the object's own colour, the object is the self-coloured inner shape.\n\nA corner bracket of a frame never passes this test, because its expanded corners land on background.\n\n**On the tests.**\n- **Test 1:** the colour‑1 frame (rows 13–19 region, columns 16–22) is drawn in 2, so it doesn't count. The 1 shape at rows 15–17 sits inside a colour‑2 frame. Checking each candidate this way: the 3‑shape at rows 3–5 is inside a 1 frame, while the 8‑shape at rows 14–16, columns 6–8 is inside a colour‑3 frame. The program will find whichever shape has corners of its own colour.\n- **Test 2:** the 9 shape at rows 13–15 lies inside the colour‑9 frame at rows 11–17, so that shape will be cropped.\n\nThe program does not rely on these readings; it applies the corner test to every object.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits two canvases:\n1. The input with every non-selected cell blanked to background, so only the chosen shape remains in place.\n2. The final cropped output.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, diagonal=True)\n    chosen = None\n    for o in objs:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        c = o[\"colour\"]\n        Y0, X0, Y1, X1 = y0 - 2, x0 - 2, y1 + 2, x1 + 2\n        if Y0 < 0 or X0 < 0 or Y1 >= H or X1 >= W:\n            continue\n        if all(g[y, x] == c for y, x in [(Y0, X0), (Y0, X1), (Y1, X0), (Y1, X1)]):\n            chosen = o\n            break\n    if chosen is None:\n        return g\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = chosen[\"box\"]\n    canvas = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    canvas[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = g[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1]\n    emit(canvas, \"Keep only the shape whose surrounding corner frame has the same colour as the shape itself.\")\n    out = crop(g, y0, x0, y1, x1)\n    emit(out, \"Crop that shape's bounding box as the output.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Test 1:** the colour‑1 frame (rows 13–19 region, columns 16–22) is drawn in 2, so it doesn't count."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the output is.** The fact sheet shows that each output is an exact crop of the input."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"I checked where each crop comes from:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 1:** the crop at (13,4) is the colour‑4 shape inside the colour‑4 frame."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"d9f37165a75b81a92bc3721657c086532e286c330b0f653bc03bb479aef49b36","task_sha256":"b06c773c65d515accc363ed352910badbea8074a225e15687f78dff4775efb47","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
